From the k149 measurement (H2/H3, Hermes test checkout ea81748). A Hermes session that moves into a worktree (`/worktree new`, the gateway turn-end re-anchor) finds the worktree untrusted: trust is an exact match on the root (`agent/skill_utils.py:584`), and nothing trusts a root by itself. Until `hermes skills trust <wt>` is run, Hermes hides **all** project skills, main's included. skilletor's move sync (k149) still writes the items, but Hermes does not load them. No banner appears mid-session.

Spec §16.5: skilletor never edits Hermes trust, and does not read the trust list. That rules out auto-trust.

## Decision for Getty

Should the move line under Hermes name `hermes skills trust <dir>` when the root is untrusted? That means skilletor reads Hermes' trust list, which §16.5 currently says it does not.

Recommendation (dispatcher): yes, read-only. Read `skills.trusted_project_dirs` and append `— run there: hermes skills trust <dir>` to the line. Never write trust. Otherwise the user sees "N item(s) updated" while every project skill has just vanished.

Decision (Getty, relayed by the hub, 2026-11-10): yes, but without reading Hermes' trust list. Under Hermes the move line names `hermes skills trust <dir>` as a hint, and spec §16.5 stays as it is. Implement after k149 (same code path), as its own commit.

Worker (k152) design: hint only in moveCheckout's sync branch, ctx.harness === "hermes" (only the Hermes prompt hook sets it; Claude's CwdChanged/PostToolUse and Codex unchanged byte-for-byte), and only when the move's sync added or updated a skill Hermes loads from that checkout: a hermes:skills/* copy or the codex:skills/* copy in .agents/skills that Hermes reads instead (addsHermesSkill). Line tail: ' — if Hermes does not trust this project yet: hermes skills trust <root>'. Context last line: '- Hermes loads project skills only from a project it trusts; if this one is not yet, the user can run: hermes skills trust <root>' (worded as the user's step so the model passes it on rather than trusting a repo's skills itself). <root> = the git top level the move synced, which is what Hermes trust matches exactly; 'hermes skills trust [path]' takes a project root (hermes_cli/subcommands/skills.py, Hermes ea81748). No hint, my call: no-change (silent, nothing to append to); trust request and config/cannot-sync error (nothing written, so nothing for Hermes to hide, and a second, different trust command beside 'skilletor trust' would confuse); removal only; a sync that changed only Claude items or agents. Under Hermes the user never sees systemMessage (plugin logs it, message_of); the context is what reaches the model, so that's the channel that counts. Trust list never read (§16.5 unchanged). Perl: no hooks, nothing to change.

Found outside the card (not fixed): (1) SessionStart under Hermes has the same gap, read from source, not measured: the CLI prints the banner (cli.py:1781 show_banner, whose untrusted-project notice counts SKILL.md files on disk) before on_session_start, which runs at the first turn's system-prompt build (agent/conversation_loop.py:864). So a session started in a fresh, untrusted worktree gets skilletor's 'N item(s) updated' with no banner warning, since the skills weren't on disk when the banner counted. Also §16.5's 'Hermes itself tells the user in its banner' holds only for skills already on disk at start; for the docs pass. (2) The hint only rides on the move's own answer. A later sync that installs into the same checkout (the background interval sync's pending report, e.g. after 'skilletor trust <src>' following a trust-request move, or the CLI) says nothing about Hermes trust. (3) skills/skilletor/references/sync.md's Codex/Hermes move bullet doesn't mention the hint; left for the docs-wide pass to keep this change minimal.

